# Next.js standards — every task, no exceptions

Before writing or reviewing Next.js/React code, follow
`~/.claude/skills/nextjs-standards/SKILL.md`. These bars apply to **bugfixes,
refactors, copy, and one-line changes**, not only new features. Do not skip
them because the task looks small.

## Non-negotiable

- **Server Components by default.** `'use client'` only on the leaf that needs
  state, effects, or browser APIs.
- **Rendering:** pick SSG / ISR / RSC / SSR / CSR per page on purpose. Never
  default to SSR. Do not cache data whose staleness would be a correctness bug.
- **DRY / KISS / YAGNI:** reuse existing `lib/`, schemas, and UI. No abstraction
  for a case that does not exist yet. Logic in `lib/`, routes validate +
  authorize + delegate, UI stays presentational.
- **Security:** validate all input server-side (Zod). Check session and
  permission in the handler, not only in the UI. SSRF-safe fetches. No secrets
  in client bundles or error messages.
- **A11y:** semantic HTML, keyboard/focus, labels, contrast. No clickable `div`.
- **UX states:** loading (skeleton), empty, error (retry when retryable),
  success — for any async surface you touch.
- **TypeScript strict.** No `any` without a one-line why.
- **Tests** match existing convention (`lib/` unit tests, route permission
  branches, critical E2E).

## Before calling the task done

Reused an existing util instead of re-deriving one. Client island scoped to
what needs it. Inputs validated and authorized in the handler. Loading / empty /
error covered if you touched async UI. Tests updated when behavior changed.
